        "title": "Different forms of hospitality",
        "text": "As a British social anthropologist, I once spent a year in Moldova, in Eastern Europe, studying everyday life in the country. I lived with a Moldovan family so that I could see from the inside how people managed their lives. I had a wonderful time and made many new friends. What I saw is, of course, based on my own experience at a particular place and time.\n\nI often found it surprisingly hard to see life there through the eyes of a Moldovan. This was because the people I met were extremely hospitable, and I was always treated as an honoured guest. My hosts wanted me to enjoy myself, and not to get involved in shopping, cooking or other domestic chores. Most mornings I was encouraged to go out and explore the city, or do my research, and when I came back later I found that my elderly landlady and her sister had travelled across the city on buses to the central market and brought back heavy loads of potatoes, a whole lamb or other large quantities of food.\n\nI was often invited to people's homes, and was always offered food as soon as I arrived. Most of the adults I met loved inviting friends, family, neighbours, colleagues and even strangers into their homes, where they gave them food, drink and a lively, hospitable atmosphere. Hosts hurried to serve their guests as well and as quickly as possible. When a household was expecting guests, large amounts of food were prepared in advance, usually by the women. The wine had already been made, generally by the men, who were also responsible for pouring it. Unexpected visitors were still offered as much food and drink as the household could provide at the time.\n\nWhen I was there, food was not always easy to buy. Grocery shops tended to be quite expensive and hard to find, so people usually shopped in markets instead. As few of the people I knew had cars, most of them had to make frequent trips to the market on foot or on crowded buses. People regularly travelled to several places to buy food and other necessities. People in the city also took part in complicated exchanges of food with their home villages.\n\nThere were many similarities with my experience of Russia during visits in the 1990s. There too, I found that people often made an enormous effort to provide very generous meals for guests. In fact, my Russian hosts seemed to feel that they could only succeed as hosts if their guests tried all the courses of the meal and ate far more than they normally would.\n\nMy impression was that there was a clear, generally accepted understanding of how hosts should behave. They were expected to provide large amounts of food and to make sure that guests ate a great deal. All the chores – the shopping, the preparation and the washing up – were the responsibility of the hosts, and a guest's offer to help was usually politely refused. Guests were unlikely to be allowed into the kitchen.\n\nIn England, the roles of host and guest tend to be rather different, in ways that some people might welcome and others regret. The two roles are less strictly defined, as the English move towards more casual ideas of hospitality than in the past. Perhaps to make guests feel at home, they may be invited into the kitchen to talk, and an offer to help with the cooking may well be accepted. Although cooking was traditionally women's work, these days far more men either help with the food preparation or take charge of it.\n\nIn general, guests are expected to eat as much, or as little, as they like. So many people are on a diet that this is accepted as a good enough reason for not eating much. Hosts do not usually feel that their food, their cooking skills or their hospitality are being criticised if a guest refuses a second helping. And after the meal, a guest who offers to help with the washing up may be disappointed to find that the offer is accepted! Unexpected visitors will probably be offered a cup of tea or coffee, and perhaps a biscuit, but offering them food is not seen as essential."
